所以你不能仅仅说你作为临床心理学家的全部角色就是做面对面的治疗。这太简化了，因为正如你刚才强调的，安全保障问题可能会从中产生。此外，长期维持这种负荷是非常巨大的。人类的问题不是机器的问题。你知道，机器不需要休息，但人类需要。这只是一些问题。但随着时间的推移，专业角色变得越来越孤立、精简。这种情况在我们的教育心理学领域也发生了。

25
00:15:17.000 --> 00:16:03.000
We historically spent most of our time supporting head teachers. We would do lots of dropping sessions, just checking in on people. We knew they were going through a tough time. and we would have the autonomy and competency to apply our specialized knowledge in a way we seem fit. And then with, I don't want to get into it now, because people have heard me speak about this. The Children and Families Act changed a lot of that towards assessment-driven things. And this is another aspect of moving away from communities of practice, but competency and autonomy are incredibly important for employee well-being. Now, I think we spend a lot of time looking at entry-level employees and not seeing how they progress through the system.
我们过去大部分时间都在支持校长。我们会进行很多走访，只是检查一下人们的情况。我们知道他们正在经历困难时期。我们有自主权和能力以我们认为合适的方式应用我们的专业知识。然后，随着——我不想现在深入讨论，因为人们已经听过我谈论这个了——《儿童与家庭法案》将很多东西转向了以评估为驱动的事情。这是远离实践社区的另一个方面，但能力和自主权对于员工的幸福感极其重要。现在，我认为我们花了很多时间关注入门级员工，却没有看到他们是如何在系统中进步的。

26
00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:59.000
Entry-level employees need more hand-holding, so to speak, less autonomy, but they still need some level of autonomy in order to assimilate that new identity. And if you come into a role where everything is siloed and everything is, you know, procedure-driven and you only follow a screen, script, there's no growth there. There's no autonomy. So without autonomy, there's no learning. There's just, well, machine learning. You learn to replicate what the script tells you. And many jobs are falling into that trap. You know, the famous one being the call center stuff, they literally follow a script. And you get promoted based on how well you know that script. But you don't get promoted on the soft skill stuff. How do you manage conflict? How do you know, if you had a call center person who you said to them, some organizations do open up to this, but you say,
入门级员工需要更多的引导，也就是更少的自主权，但他们仍然需要一定程度的自主权来同化那种新的身份。如果你进入一个角色，一切都是孤立的，一切都是程序驱动的，你只遵循屏幕上的脚本，那里就没有成长。没有自主权。所以没有自主权，就没有学习。只有——嗯，机器学习。你学会复制脚本告诉你的内容。许多工作都陷入了那个陷阱。你知道，最著名的是呼叫中心的工作，他们字面上遵循脚本。你因为对脚本的熟悉程度而获得晋升。但你不会因为软技能而获得晋升。你如何处理冲突？你知道，如果你有一个呼叫中心人员，你对他们说——有些组织确实对此持开放态度——但你说，

27
00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:49.000
we don't care if it takes two hours to do a call as long as that customer's happy, then that, you know, that's the soft market inside. It starts to filter out and, you know, you have good experiences and then that becomes your reputation. Whereas if you go through a script, like, we want you to get through 600 calls. That is the quantitative, reductive end of it. And organizations leaders, business leaders need to find out where their balance lies. You know, there's not just one way to do business. And if we're going back to the heart of this podcast, well-being, I would argue less on the metrics and more on the humanistic side of it. It's better to have five small companies where everyone's happy than one massive company where you're constantly worried about churn.
“我们不在乎打一个电话需要两个小时，只要客户满意就行”，那么，你知道，这就是软市场的内部。它开始过滤出来，你知道，你有很好的体验，然后这就成了你的声誉。而如果你走脚本，比如“我们希望你完成600个电话”，那就是量化的、还原论的终点。组织领导者、商业领袖需要找出他们的平衡点在哪里。你知道，做生意不止一种方式。如果我们回到这期播客的核心——幸福感，我会说少关注指标，多关注人文方面。拥有五家每个人都快乐的小公司，比拥有一家你不断担心人员流失的大公司要好。

